but first the prime minister says that a  new variant of covid is causing cases to  spread more quickly and the christmas  plans have to be cancelled  to avoid the nhs being overwhelmed so  how much of a threat does the new strain  pose to hospitals where we'll ask that  question now to  the bma's chair of council dr chand  nadpal thanks very much for coming with  us  uh today so the prime minister said  yesterday hospitalizations are rising  in london the south east and the east of  england what are you seeing  well today we've just uh published the  results of a major  survey of doctors experiences uh in the  nhs  over the last week and it in fact paints  a really bleak picture  doctors are saying they're overwhelmed  they've seen a massive surge  in covet cases one in three saying even  more  than during the first wave but also a  massive surge in non-coveted patients so  that  they're actually saying they don't have  the capacity the ability  to manage both and 1910 say they're not  able to provide patients with the care  they need now you know last week there  were 44 patients who had to be re-routed  away from their local hospital to more  remote sites because the hospital just  didn't have the capacity  to meet their urgent needs two hospitals  in london  declared a critical incident which  occurs when a hospital  feels it cannot manage acute and  critical care  that it's being asked to provide so this  is a serious condition  situation a situation that can only be  helped  if we can reduce the numbers of covet  patients  that are occupying hospital beds we've  seen a massive increase i think nearly  about  one in five extra beds occupied with  coveted patients over the last week  what i don't understand though is that  the nightingale hospitals that we built  during the first wave  are effectively still on standby why  don't we just increase capacity if it's  needed  i think the biggest limiting factor is  workforce  uh because we have got unfortunately we  entered the pandemic with a shortage  of doctors and nurses i think about 80  000 vacancies in the nhs  you you just can't have a doctor or  nurse in two sites at once  so so what what's probably better is to  try and make sure that  we have as many drops than nurses  available on single sites  to be able to provide care remember it  isn't just about covert patients  there are large numbers of patients who  do not have covid  that also needs to be looked after they  can also have urgent needs whether it's  cancer  whether it's heart attacks or other  acute respiratory conditions  that we see in the winter so let's not  forget their needs  too and that's the problem we had in the  first wave we had i think  around 12 000 excess deaths because  patients without covet weren't able to  be seen so it's important when we have a  shortage of workforce  we make sure they can provide the most  care  that they can within the environment  available  we of course learned about this new  variance  of covert at the press conference  yesterday that pretty sobering news  conference that the prime minister said  that could be 70 more transmissible how  concerned are you  by these reports about the new variant  well actually we were concerned  at the time that the prime minister  announced the five-day relaxation  for christmas because the science told  us then that the virus was spreading  week on week it just did not follow the  science to relax the rules last  wednesday before the new variant was  announced  it was obvious that the infection was  spreading at a dangerous level the nhs  was  in a dangerous state and again the  science wasn't followed  when in fact we should have reversed the  christmas relaxation rules so in fact  whilst there is a new variant we didn't  need the new variant  to reverse these rules and the public  could of course have been given greater  notice  but there's no doubt it was the right  and necessary  uh a decision now the important point to  make is that whilst it is a new variant  the way in which you control spread of  the uh of the virus  including this new variant is exactly  the same it's about continuing  stringent measures to maintain physical  distancing of two meters and a more  stringent use of masks and also  hand washing that the same rules apply  and i think one of the problems we've  seen over the last few weeks  is that those rules have not been  adhered to in a stringent way  i think that will require the government  in the future to take more  uh action to make sure we don't see  scenes of crowded  streets in london with choppers because  that is the sort of way in which this  virus is spreading  you talking there about compliance about  people following the rules about the  scenes of crowded shopping streets  we of course saw those scenes of people  piling onto the trains didn't we  uh to try and leave london um when the  uh new restrictions came in what would  you  say to people who are thinking about  breaking the rules over the festive  period and you said that more  action from the government is needed  what do you mean by that  well what i mean is just making sure  there's crowd control and measures like  that i mean  i would just ask the question you  wouldn't see those scenes in east asian  countries that have managed pandemics  well in the past  that's what i'm talking about just  making sure that we have uh um  clear um arrangements so that people can  be  uh separated but i think it's more than  rules what what i think is really  important for the public to understand  is this is  an unforgiving virus and in fact you  know uh  whilst christmas uh is obviously going  to be ruined for many  uh the worst thing we could possibly be  doing is unknowingly  infected our loved ones infecting people  who are older in our families  who have underlying conditions only to  find that two weeks later in the new  year  they end up with serious illness in  hospital fighting for their lives  now that's not being alarmist that's  unfortunately the way this virus behaves  and the worst thing about this virus is  that one in three people may not even  know they're carrying the virus so you  may actually be passing it on  unknowingly so i think what the  important message for the public is  please understand this is a serious risk  and when we talk about the nhs and we  say you know let's reduce pressure on  the nhs please let's also remember  that the nhs isn't some remote  disconnected service  it's a service that any of us may need  to use  uh in if there's an urgent need and none  of us want to be  in a position where the health service  cannot be there for you when you need  because it's full it's at full capacity  and is then forcing you to be uh  transported to some more remote site  with all the delays that's involved  so please let's understand that saving  and protecting each other  and looking after each other is also as  important or really  will result in protecting our national  health service  it's been a pretty depressing weekend  for all of us  you know listening to you is extremely  sort of sobering as well  so let's talk about the light that there  is at the end of the tunnel which of  course is  the vaccine doctor's gp is expected to  play a really key  role in getting that vaccine out how  quickly do you think it can be rolled  out to the population  well you know we are working flat out  i'm a gp and my practice was one of the  first  in in the pilots to start uh on on  tuesday we survey a very large  um black and asian ethnic minority  population that have been hit really  hard  by this virus and many did not survive  uh during the  last year and so it was really i have to  say it was  positive it was very heartening to see  so many patients  uh come along and be vaccinated and of  course these were patients who were much  older who were most at risk and and  we've been working seven  days a week eight a.m to eight pm and in  fact many doctors staying there right  and through till about 10 o'clock at  night  so we are working flat out it will take  unfortunately  three to four months or enough of the  population  to be to be vaccinated that's what the  projections suggest  uh but what i i and therefore there is  light at the end of the tunnel  and so i hope people the public can  understand  and be patient just just just um you  know carry on being  responsible follow the rules and  measures  because you will be if all goes well in  a few months  be able to mix with family friends and  relatives so the vaccination program is  critical  but i'm actually very proud of the way  in which healthcare workers are  delivering this program  gp practices in the community many  hospital sites  but i think the olds have to be uh  recognized that to deliver this  in in this sort of short space of time  will mean huge amounts of staff time  and for some of those stuff they won't  be able to be carrying on  without with their normal roles uh but  but yes there is  light at the end of the tunnel at the  moment with the vaccine  it does feel like that's the one  difference the one shrink of light  compared to the last  uh wave the first wave that at least we  can see  an exit route now and dr chairman paul  thank you very much for being on the  program as always good to talk to you  thank you
 

